 "dotnet_build_calls": 0, "edit_calls": 1, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "final_workspace": "/workspace/openbench-ab/results/omp-screen5-b2/omp-33/omp-33/cells/without/trig-omp-multistep/5/workspace", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "omp/17.1.1 (/workspace/pi-check/cache5/bin/omp/5d4f1cf6b5b6379d6c66c9dd0e482aaa0860513e/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "The bug: `int(19.99 * 100)` \u2192 `int(1998.9999\u2026)` = 1998 (float truncation). Fix at the source by converting via the decimal string representation with half-up rounding.\npytest isn't installed, so I'll run the test functions directly, plus the rest of the test directory.\nThe bug was in `to_cents` in `ledger/currency.py`, not in the totals code. It did `int(amount * 100)`, and floats can't represent prices like 19.99 exactly: `19.99 * 100` comes out as `1998.9999\u2026`, and `int()` cuts that down to 1998. That lost a cent on 19.99, 0.29 and 4.35, so every test in `test_totals.py` came out one cent short.\n\n**Fix:** `to_cents` now converts the price through its decimal string form (`Decimal(str(amount)) * 100`) and rounds half-up to a whole cent. Half-up matches the rounding already used in `discounts.py` and `tax.py`. `Line.cents()` is the only caller, so nothing else needed changing.\n\n**Verification:** pytest isn't installed here, so I ran every `test_*` function in `tests/` directly. All 4 tests in `test_totals.py` and `test_report.py::test_format` pass.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 6, "replies_o